00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.250
- [Chuck] Pedro Duran
Monteleone wants to know this:

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.160
What can Drake's equation
tell us about aliens?

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.230
- Oh, so Drake equation was a way

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:30.060
to organize our ignorance of the universe

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.020
when we're trying to figure out

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.760
if we can communicate with
intelligent aliens in space.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.980
It's named after Frank Drake.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.330
He first wrote this equation,

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.810
I think it was the late '60s,
might have been early '70s.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.230
You start out with the
number of stars in the galaxy

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.940
and then you say, well what
fraction of those have planets,

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.930
what fraction of those that
have planets have life,

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.000
what fraction of those
have intelligent life,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:52.833
what fraction of those

00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.780
have intelligent life with technology,

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.640
what fraction of those might actually

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.790
be listening for us at any given moment.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.460
You start hacking away at this number

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.300
and what's left at the end is an estimate

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.890
for how many civilizations are out there

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.610
that you might want to talk to.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:12.610
And, each one of these terms,
is an astrophysical question.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:13.600
You can back it up further

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.650
and say, what is the rate
the stars are being born

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.160
because that will tell you how many stars

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.820
you have to search for.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.940
So, when you put all these terms together,

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.831
you get an estimate of how many

00:02:23.831 --> 00:02:26.340
intelligent civilizations
might be out there.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.520
And right now, in one of my recent books,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.110
we actually give the very
latest calculation for this

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.150
and there's like, billions, so we're good.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.150
- Do we really wanna talk
to billions of other people?

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:38.820
- Yeah, I don't know if you,

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.890
or tell them where you
live, that would be bad.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.560
You don't give strangers,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.900
who are your own species,
your email address,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.190
much less sending the
return address to Earth

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:50.290
out to aliens across the galaxy.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.280
- Whew, alien stranger danger.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.260
Alien stranger danger!

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.093
- And Chuck, you know what

00:02:54.093 --> 00:02:55.760
the last term in the Drake equation is?

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:00.120
It's how long the intelligent civilization

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.350
might be around at all.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.330
It could be that achieving
high intelligence,

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:08.103
you now know enough how to
render yourself extinct.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.980
So, just because you became
intelligent with technology,

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.240
doesn't mean you will keep
that for all the rest of time.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.440
Maybe you have an expiration date.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:19.990
- Well, hopefully, we don't push that

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.910
date up further than it has to be.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.160
All right, let's move on, Neil.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.126
- Bring it on.

