00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.690
- [Chuck] Don Rihm wants to know this:

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.880
How likely is the theory of panspermia

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.100
for the genesis of life on Earth?

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.710
- It's really good.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.410
If you look at microbes, not all microbes,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:48.170
some microbes are just
fine in the high radiation,

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:53.130
low temperature, dehydrating
conditions of space

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.080
and we have them here on Earth.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.220
And you say, why do you have
this ability to resist this?

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.470
That's not a natural force

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:03.690
operating on your
evolutionary history here.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.733
But you would need that getting
through the vacuum of space.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:11.960
Asteroid impacts kick rocks
into interplanetary space

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.520
and they travel and they
land on other planets.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.050
There's some number of
tonnage of rocks on Earth,

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.980
that actually came from Mars,

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.180
came from other planets
in the Solar System,

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.023
so panspermia is the movement of microbes

00:04:24.023 --> 00:04:25.520
from one planet to the other

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.660
in the nooks and crannies of the rocks,

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.710
especially in the crannies. (laughs)

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:35.320
- Panspermia, the Earth
got banged by a comet

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.270
and impregnated with life.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:38.593
It's beautiful.

